Z||000003||Dave Gunsul||11-07-2001||11:03 AM||||The real reason: because the future plans of the Fiero threatened GM's precious vette but<BR>officially:<P>1.Underutilization of the Fiero assembly plant.<P>2.Small progected growth in the two seat sports car market.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><BR>This small grow was predicted because of the rising insurance costs. Of course if you look around today you'll notice that there's<BR>a lot more two seat sports cars than ever before. This was another brilliant piece of reasoning by the GM bean counters. &lt;heavy sarcasm intended&gt;  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>3. Sales were down 38% in 88 but the car was <BR>still selling more units than were projected every year of it's production run.<BR>That 38% may seem like a lot but the projections took into account the increasing<BR>ammount of competition from the imports so that's why it was still selling more than the projected sales. Obviously that drop in sales reflects that the car never fully recovered from the engine bay fire reports<BR>even though GM went out of their way to recall all potential problem cars. They even<BR>had dealers instruct people on proper oil<BR>checking and maintenance just to make sure.<P>Oh well, most people will never know what they missed.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||4
Z||000004||terryk||11-07-2001||11:51 AM||||Commander Gunsul said it best. No money to be made continuing. The production equipment could be better utilized elsewhere. <P>A business decision. We may not have liked it but it was the right decision for the company.<P>And of course the fact that the Corvette group was terrified that the 89 Fiero would be 2 seconds faster than the Vette from 0-60MPH and had a top speed of 300 MPH while getting 40MPG with an insurance rating of an econo-box.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 11-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||falconhulk||11-07-2001||11:55 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by terryk:<BR><B>Commander Gunsul said it best. No money to be made continuing. The production equipment could be better utilized elsewhere. <P>A business decision. We may not have liked it but it was the right decision for the company.<P>And of course the fact that the Corvette group was terrified that the 89 Fiero would be 2 seconds faster than the Vette from 0-60MPH and had a top speed of 300 MPH while getting 40MPG with an insurance rating of an econo-box.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 11-07-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>300 mph and 40mpg?  Then they should have been scared!  That would be the greatest sports car ever invented.  lol!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Pheagey||11-07-2001||02:20 PM||||word,<BR>what engine swas it to have? (the '89).<P>------------------<BR>'88 coupe, 5 speed: Status: destiny unknown.<BR>Broken down: 3 times<BR>Broken into: 2  times (w/i 2 days).<BR>Killed: '97 Civic LX Auto,<BR>Killed by: '93 Civic EX 5 speed,<BR>Http://www.LithiumEffects.com/car.html (B4 vandalism)||||reg||1
Z||000007||87GTBro||11-07-2001||03:03 PM||||I read in a newspaper article that the fiero project was shut down because the sales dropped 38% just like Dave said.<P>Sounds sorta familiar.  They are using the same reason for dropping the Firebird/Camaro from the lineup.  GM says the market is not open for sports cars anymore.  Most of the sales are in the SUV market.<P>I think it is a bunch of bureaucratic nonsense.  There are too many politicians in power at GM.  If they would only realize that 80% of the population who purchase sports cars are under the age of 30, maybe they'll realize that if they find ways to increase efficiency of production, they can lower the retail on the car and, therefore, sell more of them.<P>I would have a brand new Trans Am right now if I could afford one.  (I'd keep one of the Fieros, of course)  But NOOOOooo, we have to go and throw them all away!  Now EVERYBODY is going to be driving a mustang!  It's the only option left!  Either that or riced up imports with illegal performance packages (i.e. NOS)<P>I would fight to the death to protect the firebird/camaro if I was working for GM.  They've been around forever!  Now the mustang is going to be known as the only long-lasting production car.<P>Just burns me up!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/87GTBrosig_3.gif">  <BR>AOL IM: GTFiero86Dan<BR>'86 GT 4 speed<BR>'87 GT Auto||||reg||1
Z||000008||ChopTop||11-07-2001||03:21 PM||||If you want it straight from GM I'd send them an email or write them a letter asking them why.  You could also do a search on the Internet for documentation.  Also, sometimes various Fiero clubs include newspaper articles from the late 80s announcing the Fiero demise.  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Dave Gunsul||11-07-2001||11:28 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by terryk:<BR><B><P>And of course the fact that the Corvette group was terrified that the 89 Fiero would be 2 seconds faster than the Vette from 0-60MPH and had a top speed of 300 MPH while getting 40MPG with an insurance rating of an econo-box.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 11-07-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually they were terrified about the aluminum turbo charged V6 Fieros that they were experimenting with (two of which still exsist today) and the V8 Fieros that Pontiac<BR>was also experimenting with.<BR>With either power plant the Fiero would have been faster, handled better, gotten better mileage, and still cost less!<P><BR>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver<p>[This message has been edited by Dave Gunsul (edited 11-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||terryk||11-08-2001||12:13 AM||||Sorry, I think my reply was mis-read. Not my intention.<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 11-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Mike Murphy||11-09-2001||02:34 PM||||The decision to drop the Fiero was not a popular one especially with PMD employes.  I was a District Sales Manager from 82 till the demise of that great little car in 1988.<P>If you want the true facts then go buy a copy of "The History Of The Fiero"  by Gary Wittsensburg.  It's available from the Fiero store for about $29.  He tells it like it was.<P>Pontiac was lucky to build the car at all on the shoe string budget they were allowed to work with and using off the shelf mechanicals.  The first year of production was around 129,000 units and dealers could not get enough but it was more than a 12 month build.<P>The Press was nuts about it but nothing much new year 2 except the V6 and then they started to hammer it's short commings.<P>By 1988 when the revised the suspension and had the Getrag 5 speed they barely made 26,000 cars.<P>The plant utilized was the old Pontiac Assembly plant next to the Administration Building and had only been partially used.<P>The original projections that justified a profitable build on the Fiero were 100,000 annually.  Break even was around 70,000.<P>There were no bureaucratic BS politics played because it threatened the Corvette it was plain simple economics.<P>If Pontiac had been allowed to develope the car to it's full potential and spent money on it's development it may have survived.<P>One thing I do remember well is the under 25 crowd was the biggest purchaser of the car especially females on the base car.  Males caused the biggest problem with their lead feet and less than responsible driving habits and caused the insurance rates to skyrocket.  An under 25 male could afford the payments on the car but when it came to the insurance it was more than the car payment.  Many of my dealers turned away hundreds of buyers in this position.<P>When bean counters controlled the purse strings on the Fiero in the begining they were losing millions in 1980-82.  <P>It is a miracle they ever did build it but it was an example of how dedicated a group of determined CAR ENTHUSIASTS were to beat the odds and make America's only mid engine 2 seater.<P>The demise of the Camaro/Firebird?  Sad but if you can't make a profit why make it?  Solution?  I suggested this to a Pontiac employee a year ago.  Bring back the concept of the mid engine rear drive car available in both a 2 seat or 2+2 to replace the Fiero?Firebird and Camaro.<P>The more platforms a vehicle can share the more your costs can be spread to lower expenses.<P>Car manufacturers are in business to make a profit just like every other type of manufacturer of goods and services.<P>Yes I'll admit Daimlier/Chrysler builds the Viper and Prowler in low numbers but they have been bleeding for months while GM & Ford have been profitable somewhat.<P>I also blame the news media and the fickle publc at the time who trashed the car as a fire trap/ death trap.  How many times have you had to defend your Fiero because of what some idiot heard about Fieros and their habit of spontanious combustion?<P>That really boiled down to people who either abused the car or failed to properly maintain it.  Yeah about 90,000 4 bangers had possible thin wall casting of the block in a critical area but usually it was only a problem when an owner tried to over rev the engine and make a long hard corner moving most of the oil to one side of the block at a time when it needed lubrication elsewhere.  The result rod goes through block or spins a main if lucky but usually a fire resulted.<P>There was a 2.9 aluminum block developed with turbo in fact there were 2.  They did 0 to 60 in about 4.9 seconds but at the time we considered if it did come to market that the price of the package would include professional driving lessons so that the average idiot behind the wheel did not end up road kill.<P>So much for my thoughts on the subject.<P>Mike Murphy<BR>Former District Sales Manager<BR>Pontiac Motor Division<BR>St. Louis & Portland Zones||||reg||1
Z||000012||gtjeff||11-09-2001||05:53 PM||||Mike, did Pontiac ever attempt to gain GM's approval to offer a v8 model? Also, how did the suspension upgrade at a cost of $30 million ever get approved when there were already rumors of the car being canceled?<BR>How close was the aluminum space frame to<BR>making production on the Fiero?<BR>Thanks Jeff||||reg||1
Z||000013||Dave Gunsul||11-10-2001||03:13 AM||||I remember reading about a FOCOA meet that Hulki spoke to the crowd and said that the Corvette guys were always looking over the Fiero teams shoulders.<BR>Also: GM is a business and they certainly wouldn't have tooled up for the new 88 suspensions if they were going to just kill the car, they would have tryed to get away with as much money as possible by making no changes to the car.<BR>Besides all this, what ever happened to the Grand National? I remember there being a waiting list for those cars and yet they suddenly seemed to disappear after they beat the vette. If you have a waiting list then you're obviously selling cars as fast as you can build them so why did they go away?<BR>Becasue it threatened the vette that's why.<BR>BTW Pontiac was experimenting with a V8 Fiero<BR>and if you don't think that a V8 Fiero with the new 88 suspension was a threat to the Vette then you need to talk to some of the V8 owners on this forum.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000014||rigdonhome||11-10-2001||02:08 PM||||Nothing ever happens in the automotive industry that is based on sales and money alone.  Politics ALWAYS play a role, sometimes even moreso than the bean counters.  <P>The Camaro and Firebird are both simple tenths slower than the Vette right off the showroom floor...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_brow.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Scott Rigdon<BR>1988 Fiero GT, Black, 5-speed<BR>38,000 Original Miles!<BR>Web Site:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/</A> <BR>Story of buying my Fiero:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm</A> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/rear_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||4
Z||000015||WampusCat||11-10-2001||02:23 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/011284.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/011284.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||gtjeff||11-10-2001||03:37 PM||||Truth be told the 88 fiero with a v8 would be a threat to a brand new Zo6! Fiero weighs 275 pounds less than Z06,slalom times only 3-4 mph less than Zo6. <BR>GM has a mid-engine car as great as the pricey Mosler mt900 or NSX but leaves it sit dormant!!!!<BR>Pontiac is supposedly GM performance division-why arent they allowed their own unique sports car?||||reg||1
