Z||000018||Luni420||11-11-2001||03:31 AM||||Whatever dude. There is nothing out there for the price of a new Vette Z06 that could even touch it on the track or strip. NOTHING. It kept right up with the Ferrari 360 Modena in all tests and it costs a third or so of one. The new Vettes kick ass and anyone would be hard pressed to make a car out of the box for that much money to even come close to touching one. (Minus the BMW M3)||||reg||1
Z||000019||California Kid||11-11-2001||05:10 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Luni420:<BR><B>Whatever dude. There is nothing out there for the price of a new Vette Z06 that could even touch it on the track or strip. NOTHING. It kept right up with the Ferrari 360 Modena in all tests and it costs a third or so of one. The new Vettes kick ass and anyone would be hard pressed to make a car out of the box for that much money to even come close to touching one. (Minus the BMW M3)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>My '88 will beat a Z06 at the strip or road coarse, and that's exactly why Pontiac was never allowed to put a V8 in one of their production cars.  Keep in mind that Pontiac was running prototype GT Fiero's with 350's at the GM Proving Grounds in late 1986, these cars also had the prototype 88 WS6 suspension package. When the Chevrolet Corvette group saw their pride and joy getting it's butt kicked in all performance phases at the proving grounds it resulted in some very serious meetings between the heads of both divisions.<P>I'm not saying this is what killed the Fiero, but Pontiac knew it would certainly increase sales, and they could certainly up the price for the performance it delivered.<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature_7.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000020||Mike Murphy||11-11-2001||09:16 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by gtjeff:<BR><B>Mike, did Pontiac ever attempt to gain GM's approval to offer a v8 model? Also, how did the suspension upgrade at a cost of $30 million ever get approved when there were already rumors of the car being canceled?<BR>How close was the aluminum space frame to<BR>making production on the Fiero?<BR>Thanks Jeff</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000021||Raydar||11-11-2001||09:35 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>I read something on a website, not too long ago. Was supposed to be the "official" story, from beginning to end.<BR>I'll poke around and see if I can find it.<BR>Of course that will never end all the speculation by all of the "conspiracy theorists" (myself included).<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Kas,<P>The link that WampusCat posted was the one I was referring to. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000022||Kasdan||11-11-2001||10:29 AM||||Raydar:  Thanks, and it was nice meeting you yesterday!||||reg||1
Z||000023||Mike Murphy||11-11-2001||10:34 AM||||Jeff:  I know that Ed Benson was in charge of new product development on all Pontiacs during that time and he rarely shared much with the Field Sales for fear of leaks to competition however I know that a certain amount gets leaked regardless that's why there are spies.  You know the guys that do the articles for Motor Trend, Car & Driver etc.<P>A V8 in the Fiero? The engineering and product development staff try all sorts of things on a what if.  Hell in 1970 they put a steam generated power plant in a Grand Prix!<P>Would they have built it?  Probably not because of CAFE and the fact Pontiac sold GM management on the car as a 50 mpg car to help boost there CAFE rating.<P>Was Chevy worried about the Corvette with all of the possibilities on the Fiero's performance?  Maybe but Bob Stemple who was head of GM and an ex Pontiac General Manager like the car from the beginning and was one of it's biggest supporters and prior to that he had been in charge of Chevrolet.<P>I had heard about the aluminum chassis but at the time the technology was not in place for mass production.<P>As far as spending $30 million on the chassis improvements with rumors on the cars demise making the demise seem more clandestine and political is a bunch of nonsense.<P>That program was in place 2 to 3 years prior to the plug being pulled.<P>Bill Hoglund who was GM of Pontiac when the car was being developed came from the financial side but he was a car guy and helped Pontiac redefine it's image and products.  He kept a staff of well over 200 working on it when GM management said pull the plug on all unnecessary projects.  Bill was and still is one of the most respected people within GM.  His successor J. Michael Losh was also a bean counter but a car guy who listened to the marketing and engineering people with a very open mind and went on to bigger and better things within the Corporation.<P>Pontiac in 1980 was seriously being considered merged or discontinued as it's sales had dwindled to less than 300,000 annually.  By 1986 they were getting close to 3rd or 4th in the industry and were again the Performance Division of GM.  In fact Motor Trend put out an issue with every Pontiac model on the cover titled, "On a roll with Pontiac", including an interview with Mike Losh.<P>Many of the people out there reading this will want to angrily deny that it was simple economics and it was al political is BS.  When 2 of GM's most talented financial people who were in the positions they held and a President like Bob Stemple who was the first President from the engineering side of the business in over 30 years could not justify further production of a car at the rate sales had declined to it stopped.<P>I watched a car that had such an initial success I could not distribute enough to my dealers decline to the point I had to beg them to order to fill my weekly allocation and I was on the West Coast where this was our best import fighter next to the Grand Am.  When the car was selling well it would take 4 to 6 weeks to get a sold order for a customer to the dealer.  In 1988 I had stock orders not sold built and shipped from Pontiac, Michigan to Portland, Oregon in 10 days.  What did that mean? The plant had no orders and mine went straight to the top.<P>That spring in 88 there was a recall issued for all 4 cyl. Fieros built in 84 and that was the straw that broke the camel's back.<P>Pontiac's General Manager Mike Losh reluctantly made the final decision to stop production.  It was a decision all of Pontiac hated and many within GM but at the time it made sense.  A shame it was as the car with the redesigned suspension, Getrag/Muncie 5 speed was getting to be a really world class performer.  Pontiac had just spent 30 million out of it's budget and that was at their discretion not the corporation but why throw good money after bad.<P>The Fiero was in a different market & price class than the Corvette or any other GM car but the 2 seater market at the time had shrunk due to competition from Toyota, Nissan, Honda, and Subaru.<P>In the end it was a shame but so was the last GTO as will be the Firebird.  The Fiero did not last as long as the Firebird but a lot was learned on the design and manufacturing for the space frame and body panels it was constructed of.  The Fiero in 1986 had a frontal crash rating for injuries to the occupants that was as good as the full size Ford LTD.<P>Would it still be with us if Pontiac could have brought it to market all at once like the 88 model?  Hopefully but GM has had a history of bringing innovative products to the market before they are ready with disastrous results, even when their own engineers tell them not to.  Remember the gas converted 350 diesel engine that Olds introduced?  How about the X car with the steering rack problems?<P>Will we ever see a car like the Fiero again?  If we do it will be a combination of Fiero type design but on a platform that it can share with other car lines to offset production costs.  Remember the Fiero had not earned a profit for Pontiac the last 2 years of production and it was in a dedicated plant that was shared with no other GM car.  There was talk of giving Chevy a version but that idea was thrown out as it had never really been successful in the long haul with other attempts to prop up sagging sales.  Remember the Nova when Olds, Buick & Pontiac got their versions in the 70's?  Where are they now? The Omega, Apollo and Ventura?<P>Be happy we had the Fiero and if you are lucky enough to own 1 or 3 like I do just be content that you own and maintain a very significant piece of automotive history.<P>Mike Murphy||||reg||1
Z||000024||Primaris||11-11-2001||12:39 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Luni420:<BR><B>Whatever dude. There is nothing out there for the price of a new Vette Z06 that could even touch it on the track or strip. NOTHING. It kept right up with the Ferrari 360 Modena in all tests and it costs a third or so of one. The new Vettes kick ass and anyone would be hard pressed to make a car out of the box for that much money to even come close to touching one. (Minus the BMW M3)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ever hear of an RX 7?  Yes, the one they are not building any more.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000025||gtjeff||11-11-2001||03:25 PM||||Mike, thanks for your answer. <P>GM if it really wanted to could have kept the Fiero in their lineup by keeping the Pontiac plant open 1 more year then when the Saturn plant opened in KY shifted production there.<P>Saturn had plans for their own version of the Fiero and Chevy could have also built the Tandem off this platform making the car more cost efficient.<P>According to a magazine article from 1988, GM even considered shifting Fiero production to Bowling Green at a $100 million cost.<P>For 2003 GM so far is only offering 2 sports cars in the high end market-Corvette and XLR. The affordable end of the market is being ignored-poor marketing strategy when GM has a potential world class mid-engine car sitting dormant. <P>Pontiac does a good job of alienating former customers by not offering models like GTO and Fiero. They could use some marketing help.||||reg||1
