Z||000029||87GT||11-11-2001||08:12 PM||||Its sad that Pontiac is suppose to be the performance division and now that the Trans-Am is going, the only sports car they have is the GTP Grand Prix.  They got this new Vibe thing coming out but it looks like an import wagon and is going to have an import motor in its upscale version.  Pontiac definately isnt what it used to be.  Hopefully they will come out with something cool again in the next 10 years or so.  Its really nice to see somebody on here that actually worked with Pontiac so that we can get more info.  Thanks   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000030||artherd||11-11-2001||08:16 PM||||HAHHAHAHHA!<P>Thanks, I needed that.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Luni420:<BR><B>Whatever dude. There is nothing out there for the price of a new Vette Z06 that could even touch it on the track or strip. NOTHING. It kept right up with the Ferrari 360 Modena in all tests and it costs a third or so of one. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000031||terryk||11-11-2001||09:09 PM||||"Its really nice to see somebody on here that actually worked with Pontiac so that we can get more info." <P>And correct info.||||reg||1
Z||000032||California Kid||11-11-2001||10:10 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mike Murphy:<BR>Be happy we had the Fiero and if you are lucky enough to own 1 or 3 like I do just be content that you own and maintain a very significant piece of automotive history.<BR>Mike Murphy[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>********************************************<BR>Mike,<BR>Welcome to the Forum!  Thanks for sharing the perspective from the Management side, which is very accurate. I worked at the GM Tech Center from '80 to 89' on the "W" Car platform as an engineer on door systems. There was a flurry of internal Fiero talk during this period, very significant items that you have stated above. I received quite a bit of feedback from a friend on the Fiero Program over at the Entech Bldg who worked on the performance prototypes.<P>In my opinion you are very correct about the performance prototypes being too quick for the average consumer. For GM or any other Manufacturer to make and sell cars this quick (Z06 level), a free drivers performance school should be included in the price.<P>As you stated "Be happy to own one or more", they are pretty significant vehicles!<P>Best,<BR>Tom<BR><p>[This message has been edited by California Kid (edited 11-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000033||Freshj||11-11-2001||10:33 PM||||Coming stright from a guy who worked on the Fiero project. They slapped a Grand National motor in it, saw that the vette was in trouble, it went bye bye||||reg||1
Z||000034||Dave Gunsul||11-12-2001||02:08 AM||||I don't intend this as a flame but I think i'll believe Hulki Aldikacti (sp?) over<BR>a Pontiac sales guy since Hulki is basically<BR>the father of the Fiero.<BR>Besides that I will again point out that GM has erased other cars that have threatened the vette as well: the Grand National, Typhoon, and Cyclone are good examples.<BR>Also: why was the turbo Trans Am only made one year?? Why have the f-bodies (Camaro and Firebird) alwasy been kept slower than the vette?<BR>Oh well, there's really no winning this argument some people will always believe what the media and big brother GM tells them.<BR>Even Motor Trend said that when you mess with GMs flag ship (the vette) that's a big no no and you get dropped from the line up.<BR>(that was taken from a report they did on the Grand National)<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000035||Gerald||11-12-2001||08:42 AM||||Maybe instead of canceling the Fiero because chevy got scared, chevy could of had the determination to make the vette faster. A mid 80's vette was a joke. My moms mini van had nearly as much power. (200 hp) The 2.8 v6 was a joke untill pontiac got their hands on it. Some even dynoed up to 175 with minamal mods and good build quality. Even that would of been enuf to smoke an 86 vette.||||reg||1
Z||000036||Formula88||11-12-2001||10:46 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Dave Gunsul:<BR><B>I will again point out that GM has erased other cars that have threatened the vette as well: the Grand National.<BR>Even Motor Trend said that when you mess with GMs flag ship (the vette) that's a big no no and you get dropped from the line up.<BR>(that was taken from a report they did on the Grand National)<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, I tend to agree the Vette people played a hand in the Fiero's demise, but the GN stopped production after 87 because the 88 Regal was front wheel drive.  The 3800SC wasn't ready yet, and it didn't have the power anyway (at the time).  I doubt Chevy had GM kill the entire G-body platform and replace it with a FWD version to get rid of the GN.  It would have been easier to just make them stop selling GN's.  But M/T was right.  The Vette camp was furious when the GN bested it's fiberglass baby in the 1/4 mile in '87.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000037||Mike Murphy||11-14-2001||05:02 PM||||Yeah I was just a sales guy for Pontiac in the 80's....but I grew up in a Pontiac Dealership in the Midwest during the 60's and 70's.  I saw some of the best performance cars that Pontiac ever built insofar as raw horsepower.  I saw what made Pontiac 3rd in the industry in sales behind Ford & Chevrolet.  The GTO, 2+2, Grand Prix, Bonneville, Firebird, Trans AM, 389's Tri-Powers, Super Duty 455's to name a few.  I also saw the mistakes they made trying to compete with Buick & Olds in the late 70's and later.  How about the Astre?  The 1st rear drive version of the Sunbird? The 74 GTO with a Buick 350 engine based on the Ventura/Nova platform?  Bonnevilles with 350 Diesels that go bang?  The first version of the Grand Am in the 70's.  The gutless 301 turbo with lag in the 1980 Firebirds.  I drove all of those cars and few were impressive or showed much promise to build excitement as was evident in their sales although a few are worthy collectables such as the 73 Grand Am and even the 74 runt of the litter GTO. <P>Those were tough times for PMD faced with new tough emission and mandated fuel economy standards.   Try being a dealer and sell cars to the public when you can't buy gas or the price more than doubles on your gas guzzling 5,000 lb land Yacht.<P>Quality? It wasn't until the Japs started eating their lunch that GM started to wake up and do something about it but the transition took most of the 80's to do so and convert to more fuel efficient light weight vehicles.<P>Pontiac almost turned out the lights but the little Fiero was the Halo car that got them going again.  Sadly it did not live up to it's potential soon enough but it did serve a purpose in putting Pontiac back on the road to build exciting cars.<P>Yes there is a hierarchy in GM and it has been there since the days of Alfred P. Sloan who set the tone within GM.  His philosophy was to sell the public a car they could afford starting with a Chevrolet and later they could move up to a Pontiac, a Buick, Olds and finally a Cadillac as their prosperity  and life styles improved.  With the exception of the Vette that strategy still holds true today.  <P>Sorry performance enthusiasts and generation what ever you are GM did not erase cars like the Grand National, Turbo Trans Am,Typhoon and Cyclone because they threatened the almighty vette Motor Trend fiction writers avenge.  The Grand National was built on a platform that should have been scrapped 1 to 2 years before it actually did leave production for the front drive W body program.  The Grand National accounted for a small portion of the total Buick Regal platform and in fact the turbo V6 was stock piled as it was being phased out for the front drive models.  Pontiac got a few in 89 for the Trans Am Pace Car and that was it as it was an engine that was being phased out.  The Typhoon and Cyclone? Very limited production vehicles.  Why an experiment for one but mostly they were what we called "HALO" cars.  They were an excercise in developing new technology and products that would not be large volume runs but the excitement and interest created would bring buyers into the showroom who might not buy that specialty auto or truck but a more affordable practical one that was more mainstream.  I saw it work more than once.  The excited husband brings the wife in to look at the new Trans Am and after she brings him down to reality with the needs of the family they drive out in a family oriented vehicle.<P>If you think I'm trying to win an argument whatever.  I just thought some people out there might appreciate the facts as they really went into the history books at GM.<P>Mike Murphy<BR>Former Pontiac Retailer<BR>Former District Sales "MANAGER"<BR>Pontiac Motor Division<BR>Now Pontiac Enthusiast Collector<BR>ANY other BS title you care to call me but leave my mother out of it.||||reg||6
Z||000038||Little Duce Coupe||11-14-2001||05:49 PM||||It's not just Pontiac (GM) that will put a good idea to market before it is ready.<P>The T-bird, K-cars, X-cars... on and on, but the most interesting fact is I believe is that the Corvette fell into this group also.<P>If memory servrs it was first put together using off the shelf frame ideas and parts,<BR>wonder that.  It took 2-3 years before it started to become a "self aware" car.<P>The engineer almost always has a good idea, the marketing guy can see and knows how to sell it, its the bean counter who can't wait for his money that mucks it all up. <P>In the end all the bean counter sees is a name that is associated with $$ which equaled vette.<P>------------------<BR>Little Duce Coupe<BR>"You Don't Know What She Costs"||||reg||13
Z||000039||JB||11-14-2001||10:56 PM||||Mr. Murphy, I solute on both your Knowledge and your ability to not get hand cramps from Typing!!!!!!!||||reg||6
Z||000040||Dave Gunsul||11-14-2001||11:49 PM||||Did it ever occur to you that you were handed the same  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif"> that the public was handed? <BR>You should really try to read some of the interviews with Hulki before you make your<BR>stand on this issue. Although Gary Witzenburgs book is very good it doesn't contain everything about the subject.<BR>As a matter of fact it had to be revised to include the 88 model year.<BR>I will concede the Grand National issue but<BR>Gm doesn't throw away money on building limited models like the Cyclone and then discontinue it when it's selling well.<BR>The vette was humiliated by that truck and<BR>that's why it was killed. The Gm brass was opposed to the Fiero from the beginning because it was afraid that it would compete with the vette but Pontiac said that they just wanted to make a commuter car and that's<BR>what got it passed. When the car started to become something more than that it was first watched very carfully, complained about, and<BR>ultimately killed.<BR>Something about your posts strikes me as familiar that isn't you is it Eds?<BR>Anyways believe what you want.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000041||Ferrari_cdn||11-15-2001||03:15 AM||||If something CAN be better than Corvette, then GM is STUPID to not let it go on.  If the Fiero did actually outperform Corvettes, both at speed and handling, then so be it.  Maybe that would mean Corvettes go down, but at least GM now HAS something FASTER.  But no, GM has to stay with something slower.<P>Smart move I guess.||||reg||8
